   "grounded": false,
   "answer": "\u2192 **Market makers** sell options to traders, but they hate holding unhedged risk, so they buy or sell the underlying index to stay neutral.\n\n\u2192 **Positive GEX** means dealers are net-long options. When the market drops, they buy shares to protect themselves; when it rises, they sell. They act like a **shock absorber** that dampens volatility and pins price.\n\n\u2192 **Negative GEX** means dealers are net-short options. When the market drops, they have to sell more shares; when it rises, they buy more. They act like **gasoline on a fire**, amplifying moves and creating fast trends.\n\n\u2192 **What it means for SPX weeklies:** heavy positive GEX equals slow chop and pin behavior; negative GEX equals explosive momentum where small moves turn into massive runs."
  },
